<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Seat harness ~ a capability never lent by ambient authority

A seat holds only the capabilities its wall grants. Every act runs under exactly one authority, and the harness names it. Each lineage (Codex, Copilot, Claude) wears the same seat spec, so a wall keeps one meaning across them. The crew shapes and the lane law stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>>; this carrier holds the machinery beneath them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-trust-model`>>
!! The trust model ~ who holds each capability, whose authority runs each act

|! Actor |! Holds |! Runs under its own authority |! Never |
| ''Operator'' | rulings, credentials, the push of the span and `main`, every founding and custody act | anything | delegates a credential to a seat |
| ''Controller'' | the one go per push, seat assignment, the lane's merge, the read of every chunk | its own commits and pushes | executes code from a lane tree it has not read |
| ''Broker'' (`tools/commit-relay.mjs`) | fixed data acts a lead requests: a commit of named paths, a push the controller's go names, a builder worktree, a walled seat's launch | git and the launcher, from a path outside the lane | grants a go; launches a lead; touches a path outside the lane root |
| ''Lead'' | its worktree and its lane root, no git dir and no network flag (the model API rides the codex process, outside the sandbox) | edits, tests, briefs, requests to the broker | commits or pushes itself; writes the main checkout |
| ''Builder'' | its worktree alone | edits, typecheck, single-file tests | commits; reaches the network |
| ''Reader'' | nothing writable | reads | writes any file; its final message carries the hand-back |

''The trust boundary of the broker.'' The broker executes no code from a lead's tree. It runs data-only git plumbing (`write-tree`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref` with an expected old head) through a pinned absolute `git`, a fixed environment (attributes read from the empty tree), an isolated home and temp dir, and a push that runs from a throwaway repo of the broker's own to the controller's remote URL, so the lane's `.git/config` (pushurl, insteadOf, helpers) is never read, so a planted hook, credential helper, `.gitattributes` filter, submodule, poisoned `PATH`, `GIT_*` or `NODE_OPTIONS` reaches nothing. Hooks and validators run in a separate ''check seat'' behind bubblewrap (no network, a read-only root, one writable work dir) over an exact export of the tree; the broker commits only when it verifies that seat's signed receipt, which covers the tree, the check names and the check definitions; the checked tree is bound read-only for every check, and the key and the receipts live in the broker's memory alone, never on disk, because a workspace-write sandbox fences writes, not reads, and it refuses a hardlinked file. Installs run in an ''install seat'' that binds the worktree, the store, the git dir and every protected path by bubblewrap's fd binds (`--bind-fd`, `--ro-bind-fd`) over directories held open once, and reads `.npmrc` through the held worktree handle with O_NOFOLLOW (a symlink there refuses by name before any read) (scripts off, pnpmfile ignored, offline, a refused `.npmrc` key by name, `.git` read-only, the controller dir read as empty so the key and the go stay unreadable). The proofs live in `tools/commit-relay.test.mjs`. The request directory is held open by handle (`/proc/self/fd` anchoring) so a swapped `relay` redirects nothing. A new worktree is built where it stays, in a broker-owned staging directory, and the lane shows it as a symlink the broker makes through a parent directory it holds open, with rollback through the same handle. The trusted launcher must sit outside the scratch tree entirely, and the broker's own state dir is checked by real path: outside the lane, every worktree and the whole scratch lane tree, before ANY directory (relay/ included) is created. Two edges remain open: the receipt key lives in the broker process's memory, so a same-user `ptrace` of the broker would read it (the host's `ptrace_scope` and the seat's seccomp are the wall there), and the wall rests on `bwrap` being present (its absence refuses by name, never runs unwalled).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seat-spec`>>
!! The seat spec ~ one shape, rendered per lineage

A seat names its lineage, model, effort, mode, wall, seat directory under `~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/`, and its brief file. `tools/launch-codex.mjs` renders it into Codex flags and `tools/launch-copilot.mjs` into Copilot tool gates.

|! Mode |! Writes |! Network |! Commits |
| `read` | nothing (the final message carries the hand-back) | off | never |
| `research` | its seat directory | on, with search | never |
| `build` | its worktree and seat directory | off | never; its lead lands the diff |
| `lead` (Codex) | its worktree, the repo's git dir, its lane root | on | through the broker alone |

''Walls that hold by measure.'' The Codex sandbox keeps `.git` read-only inside every granted root, so no sandboxed Codex seat commits. A seat launched inside another seat's sandbox inherits that sandbox and cannot initialize its own runtime state, so the broker launches every seat a lead asks for. A Copilot build seat's tool gate may deny `node` and `pnpm`; its lead or the controller then runs the tests.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lead`>>
!! The lead ~ fresh per step, never one long session

* `tools/lane-state.mjs` holds the shape and `node tools/launch-codex.mjs --lead-step --lane-root <dir> --model <m> --effort <e> --worktree <wt>` launches the step: it reads `<lane-root>/state.json`, writes `<lane-root>/steps/step-NNN/brief.md` from the state, and launches a lead there with the lane root added so the lead can rewrite the state. The step counter spends only when the preflight passes, a missing or misshapen state refuses by name, and the lead ceiling bounds the step.
* A lead runs ONE step per launch: it reads its lane's state file (head, owed rows, live seats, worktree map), does that step, writes the state file and its hand-back, and stops. The next step launches a fresh lead over the new state.
* A step stays short: about 10 to 15 minutes or 40 tool calls. An agentic session re-sends its whole transcript on every call, so one long session grows its draw roughly with the square of its length.
* A lead dispatches and waits outside the model: the broker or the controller's watcher blocks on a seat, and the next lead step reads the result path. A lead never polls inside its own context.
* A failing test counts as work. A lead stops only at a wall, at a design question that needs an operator ruling (named with its options), or at the push go.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-broker`>>
!! The broker ~ the hands outside a walled lead

* ''The swap'': `tools/seat-broker.mjs` holds the plumbing doors, `tools/seat-walls.mjs` the check and install seats, `tools/tree-export.mjs` the filter-free tree export. ''Requests'' arrive as `req-<id>.json` files in `<lane-root>/relay/`, zero-padded and in order, one at a time; each answer lands as `res-<id>.json` before the next request runs. Unknown kinds and unknown fields refuse by name.
* ''Doors'': `commit` (named relative paths read race-proof from the worktree, the message as inline text (size-capped; the broker opens no lead-controlled file for it), an expected old head, a verified check receipt; no hook, no filter); `push` (only when the go file names the branch AND the head; never `-u`, never force; the go spends on the attempt); `worktree` (a new path under the lane root, a branch carrying the lane prefix, a filter-free export of the base tree, submodule paths left empty); `install` (the install seat over a worktree in the lane root); `worktree-remove` (a registered, clean tree, never the broker's own, never `--force`); `launch` (`build`, `read` or `research` alone, never `lead`; allowlisted models; a seat directory inside the lane root with its brief).
* ''The go'' lives outside the lane root and the worktree, at a path the controller names when it starts the broker, so a lead cannot grant its own push.
* ''The broker runs'' from a copy outside the lane and with a launcher outside the lane; a lead's edit to the lane's own copy never reaches the running broker.
* ''A crew cap'' may ride the broker: it refuses a `launch` while a seat of the same kind stays live, naming the cap, so a lead adapts by reading the answer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-spend-guard`>>
!! The spend guard ~ true usage, read where each tool writes it

* ''True usage'' reads from each Codex rollout's `token_count` events (`total_token_usage`: input, cached input, output, reasoning) and from each rollout's `rate_limits.primary.used_percent`. A seat's printed "tokens used" line understates the draw by orders of magnitude, so no tally reads it.
* ''Cached input counts.'' Caching lowers the price of a token, never its weight against a rate limit or a plan pool.
* ''The floors'': the Codex pool keeps 20% for a build, research or lead seat and 0% for a read seat; the launcher refuses at or under the floor.
* ''The used-percent lines'': a lead step refuses above 50% of the weekly pool used, a build or research seat above 60%, and a read seat stays at its floor; each refusal names the reading.
* ''The per-seat ceiling'': the launcher starts a detached supervisor that is the seat's parent: it spawns codex itself, so the child pid cannot be reused before the supervisor reaps it, and it signals only its own child. It tails the seat's own rollout (matched by the seat's own marker, which rides its prompt and which the first user-prompt event of its rollout records; a seat with no NEW token event for 200 ticks (ten minutes; a re-read of the last total does not count; `--blind-ticks N` moves the limit and the default stays 200) is stopped, and any error while watching stops it too, failing closed. The stop writes ceiling.md FIRST, then runs the ladder (SIGTERM to the group, grace, SIGKILL to the group, a wait for the exit event); a signal that throws never reads as stopped, and a watch that ends with the child alive makes the supervisor SIGKILL its own child. The relay's go and state live under the SAME fixed broker root, `<root>/go/<lane-id>/push-go` and `<root>/iso/<lane-id>`, where the lane id is the lane's base name plus a digest of its REAL path (two lanes sharing a base name never share a go or a state dir). The controller's `tools/push-go.mjs` writes a four-line go (lane id, branch, head, push destination) and the relay refuses a go whose lane or destination differs from the one it holds. No caller names a path (a go or state dir anywhere under `~/.lares-scratch`, another lane included, refuses); a made worktree stages in `.broker-worktrees/<lane-id>` under scratch (verified like the root: real directories, ours, exactly 0700), no granted root (worktree, seat dir, lane root) may equal, contain or sit inside that staging tree except the exact made worktree a build seat was handed, and a build or lead worktree outside scratch refuses. A launched seat runs in a BROKER-OWNED seat directory under that ONE fixed broker root that no caller names: `~/.local/state/lares-broker` with `seats`, `go` and `iso` inside it (moved only by the `LARES_BROKER_ROOT` environment the controller sets), outside `~/.lares-scratch` entirely, made 0700, verified by lstat on every component from the home down (real directories, ours, no group- or world-writable ancestor, the root exactly 0700), with a size bound (`LARES_BROKER_MAX_GB`, ten by default) that refuses a launch by name, a fresh `mkdtemp` seat dir per launch, and the seat dir removed after the hand-back or a refusal. A dry run creates nothing. Codex runs inside a PID namespace (`bwrap --unshare-pid --die-with-parent`, measured to nest with Codex's own sandbox on this host), so when the namespace's init dies every descendant dies, setsid or not; a live seat refuses by name where that wall cannot stand. Every supervision exit path (a throw, a clean exit, an unstopped group, a size stop) ends in one `finally` that removes the broker seat dir, or keeps it and names it in the marker while its group still lives, and a running seat's seat dir has a live byte bound (`LARES_SEAT_MAX_BYTES`, two GiB by default; over it the seat stops with verdict `size-ceiling`). The namespace contains every descendant but does not reserve the host process-group id after the child is reaped; that pid-reuse edge stays accepted. The launch message names a completion marker `done-<uuid>` the supervisor writes LAST (it carries the verdict, the exit code, the names copied and any hand-back error, and it stands even when the supervision itself fails; the wait also watches the supervisor's pid), a containment command that fails before Codex runs reads `setup-failed`, and every group signal rides one guard, `killGroup`, that refuses any pgid that is not an integer above 1 (a pid-1 test fake once reached `kill(-1)` and killed every process the operator owns; signal-spawning suites run only inside a bwrap PID namespace, and a witness reads any `process.kill` outside the guard as red), and a reused lane seat dir moves its old final.md, ceiling.md, out.md and done-* into `prev-<n>/` first. Under that root: Codex's `-o final.md`, `TMPDIR`, run.log and ceiling.md all land there, where no lead can write, and the lead names a lane seat dir the broker never runs a writer against. After the seat's process group is gone, the supervisor copies the seat's regular top-level files (each capped at 8 MiB, at most 64, never symlinks or directories) into the lane's seat dir through a held handle (an old entry unlinked, then O_NOFOLLOW|O_EXCL), so a lane dir swapped mid-run redirects nothing. Every launcher write into the lane's seat dir (the brief, attempts.log, the snapshot, the marker, the prompt) goes through one directory handle opened and verified under the lane, with O_NOFOLLOW and a single-link check; lead steps hold the lane root the same way (`steps/`, the claim and `state.json` all through it); the supervisor inherits both handles as fds. A witness test reads any pathname fs write in `launch-codex.mjs` or `lane-state.mjs` as red, and an access witness reads ANY fs call on a non-held path in the four broker files (`commit-relay.mjs`, `seat-broker.mjs`, `seat-walls.mjs`, `tree-export.mjs`) as red unless it goes through a held handle or carries the broker-owned mark (the controller's own arguments, the broker root, the staging tree, the home's credential list). The staging parent's verifier also refuses a group- or world-writable scratch dir or home. When a child exits, the supervisor SIGKILLs its whole process group at once (ESRCH is fine) and confirms the group answers ESRCH before a stop reads as stopped) and sums `total_token_usage.total_tokens`; at the ceiling (15M for a lead step, 8M for build, read and research; `--ceiling N` overrides) it sends SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, and writes `ceiling.md` in the seat directory naming the reading. The watchdog never reads `run.log`. A Copilot run reads its spend from its log's `Requests N Premium` line, and a log without one reads UNMETERED, never zero.
* ''Compaction'': every seat launches with `-c model_auto_compact_token_limit=120000` and `-c tool_output_token_limit=8000`, launcher overrides only (`~/.codex/config.toml` stays the operator's). `--canary` (read mode) writes a brief that makes about 100 small tool calls in one turn and runs under a 50000 limit; `--check-canary --seat-dir D` reads that seat's rollout and prints `COMPACTION-FIRED` (exit 0) or `COMPACTION-NOT-FIRED` (exit 1) with the event, call and token counts; the launcher snapshots the existing rollouts before it spawns the seat. Measured 2026-11-04 (Luna, low effort): compaction fired inside `codex exec` (1 event, about 100 calls, 2.9M total tokens).
* ''Reasoning effort'' moves little of the draw; context length moves most of it. Effort follows the step's difficulty, never the budget.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/owed`>>
!! Owed ~ the rows this design still lacks

# A deep read and debug pass over the whole harness once the wiki server stands (the Terra rounds continue there; the nine rounds so far found their classes by placement and authority, and the residuals named below stand open until then: the host process-group id after a reap, the sampled size ceiling, the ptrace and seccomp assumptions around the in-memory receipt key).
# The swarm's live controls move out of flat files into the live Lararium wiki once it stands. This row holds the design only; nothing builds until the wiki stands.
## ''The tiddlers.'' Four families, each a tiddler the controller's hand signs: the ''crew cap'' (one tiddler per seat kind, a count), the ''push go'' (branch and head, one tiddler per push, spent on the attempt), the ''lane state'' (the fields of `state.json`: head, owed rows, live seats, worktree map, next step, step counter), and the ''seat roster'' (one tiddler per seat: lineage, model, mode, seat directory, live or done, and its spend readings from #/the-spend-guard).
## ''Who writes.'' The controller's hand signs the cap and the go. A lead writes the lane state and the roster rows for its own seats, and its signature names the lead. A seat reads through the capability its wall grants: a read seat reads the cap and its own roster row; a lead reads the lane state; the broker reads the go and nothing else. The watchdog writes the roster's spend reading and `ceiling.md` equivalents under its own signature.
## ''What each carries.'' Every tiddler carries an author and a history, so a cap or a go answers who set it and when in the causal order, never by a file mode. A go binds branch AND head, and the broker verifies the signature before the push, then writes the spent mark.
## ''What stays out of the wiki.'' The check receipt's key and the broker's isolated state stay on the controller's disk: a lead's capability never reaches them, and a wiki write never substitutes for the receipt the check seat signs.
## ''Migration.'' The flat files (`state.json`, the go file, the relay directory) keep serving until the wiki stands; the move then runs as one rite per family, the file read once into a tiddler and burned, with the launcher and broker reading the tiddler through the same capability a seat uses. A causal-island boundary holds any seat that cannot reach the wiki: it falls back to its last signed state and reports the stale reading, never a guess.
## ''Open questions for the operator.'' Whether a lead may sign its own roster rows or only the controller may; whether the crew cap lives per lane or per house.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-swarm-slices`>>
!! Row 7's slices ~ order, state and the design of S3 to S6

The wiki-daemon key cap stands in six slices, each red-first with its control. The operator ruled it at standing (2026-11-04) and ruled the ward's reach (2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>). The founding waits on `S1` to `S3`, `S2b`, `S3-pre-b` and `S3b`; `S4` to `S6` follow the standing.

|! Slice |! Holds |! State, 2026-11-05 |
| `S1` | the swarm tiddler model and the hand-only verbs | built; rides the `S2` lane |
| `S2` | the ward: a `ward-slots.bin` tree under each face, a fresh unlock per act, passkey refused | built with its cold-read cures and the marker cure (the product founds no face from a marker file); on its lane, CI green at its earlier head |
| `S2b` | the browser root under the ward | owed before the rite |
| `S3a` | the co-sign judge, the self-answer cure, `ask --detail`, the blinded per-face vessel tag | built on its own lane |
| `S3-pre-b` | the founding and join wiring of the vessel tag, default `required` | building on the `S3a` lane; founding-critical |
| `S3b` | the gate hook that calls the judge | owed before the rite, after `S2` |
| `S4` | the capability channel and the AF_UNIX probe | designed below |
| `S5` | vessel-signed roster and receipt, the broker swap | designed below |
| `S6` | canon | designed below |

!!! Live holes the scouts found

Each cure rides the slice named.
* `S3`: the intent plane approves a SELF-answer. Any enrolled device signs an answer, and the read checks only that the answer's executor equals the acting atom, so the asker approves its own ask.
* `S4`: `tools/launch-codex.mjs` hands the supervisor fds 3 and 4 without close-on-exec, and the bwrap spawn probably carries them into bwrap and Codex. Treat it as red until a witness measures that the seat sees only fds 0 to 2.
* A same-uid process can open `session:open` with hand `cli` and wear the unlocked face (the connector defaults the hand to `cli`, and `cli` maps to the `verb` profile, which reaches every hand-only act, since the socket refuses hand-only acts for the `seat` profile alone). The ward closes that for bound acts. The lift posture for user wikis stays advisory by ruling.
* Node has no `socketpair(2)` and cannot pass an fd to an unrelated live process.

!!! S3 ~ the co-sign

* A pure judge, `judgeCosign({view, intentId, requester, projection, standing, digest})` in `lararium-mesh/src/ward-cosign.ts`. The device count reads the projection's signed admission fold: zero or one device answers `none` (the fresh unlock alone), two or more answer `cosign`.
* A co-sign approves only when the answer carries a signature by an enrolled device that differs from the requester and whose standing reads `stands`; the ask's signer equals the requester; the ask's detail equals the digest of (act, face, wiki, head cid); the answer stands uncontested; and the intent stands unspent. Set-aside devices count toward the total and never co-sign. The co-signing device fresh-unlocks too.
* `ward-kapae-lower`, `ward-house-cap` and `ward-push-go` register as single-effect face intent verbs with audience `asks`. The outcome slot spends the intent.
* The gate (an `S2` ward act) calls the judge. When a co-sign applies, it writes the ask and refuses `ward-awaiting-cosign: <ask cid>`: no clock, no timeout, no fallback. An offline second device refuses for good until it answers.
* Reds: a self-answer must not approve; a digest mismatch, an aside answerer and a contested pair refuse; a replay after the outcome refuses `spent`; with two devices and a peer that never answers, a thousand retries stay `ward-awaiting-cosign`. Controls: a second device approves, and a one-device fleet lands on the unlock alone. A table-driven case runs over node and browser vessels.

!!! S4 ~ the capability channel

* The swarm verbs form their own `swarmDoor` and never join the session acts the custody socket admits, so a session of any hand (`cli`, `tab`, `seat`) that reaches for a swarm act refuses `act-rides-the-custody-door`.
* A ward act, `swarm-holder-start {kind: broker|watchdog}`, makes the daemon spawn the holder from a pinned tool path with `stdio[3]="pipe"` (libuv's socketpair) through an `sh` double fork, so the holder reparents to init: the holder stands beside the daemon, never under it. A ppid-chain witness proves the daemon pid sits in no seat's or holder's ancestry, with a direct-child spawn as its red control. Only if that witness cannot hold does a daemon-minted bearer ride the broker's inherited pipe, zeroed after use.
* The daemon identifies a holder by which end of the pair spoke, and no token rides a frame. The broker holds go-read, go-spend, receipt-sign, receipt-verify and cap-read; the watchdog holds roster-write alone. An EOF or a dead daemon reads `cap-gone`, and a restart mints fresh pairs.
* The probe measures first. A fixture server (never the daemon) takes a connect under bwrap plus Codex's sandbox with the network off and workspace write (`codex sandbox linux` or `codex debug landlock`; no model call). `tools/seat-socket-probe.expect.json` pins the measured result, and the hermetic arms assert both outcomes: a connect that succeeds without a capability gets `no-cap`, and a denied connect gets EPERM. Until the probe measures, a Codex seat counts as able to reach the socket, so the verbs ride the fd and never the face-wearing socket path.
* The fd cure closes fds 3 and up before `exec bwrap`.

!!! S5 ~ the broker swap

* A client over the inherited capability fd, `tools/swarm-door.mjs`, carries the frames: `go-read` (the daemon verifies the hand's `wardSig` and the co-sign outcome, spends once and returns branch and head), `receipt-sign` (a vessel signature in the domain `swarm-receipt`, writing `receipt/<tree>`), `receipt-verify`, `cap-read` and, for the watchdog, `roster-write`. A locked custody answers `swarm-vessel-locked`.
* The flat go file, the relay's HMAC key, its receipts map and the go lane directory retire (`push-go.mjs` goes; the isolated state stays). With no capability fd the relay refuses at start, before it makes a broker root, with "no standing daemon: swarm controls need the founded wiki", and writes nothing. The watchdog stops a seat on a capability EOF, and the ceiling still rides the launch argument.
* Reds, against a stub daemon pair end: a push whose go came only from the stub pushes (control); no record gives "no push-go"; a wrong head, a replay after the spend and a forged receipt all refuse; with no fd the relay exits non-zero and the broker root shows nothing new. A retirement witness (`tools/swarm-retired-witness.sh`) greps for `push-go`, `createHmac`, a `randomBytes(32)` key in the relay, the go file and the go lane directory, with a positive fixture. On the node side, a receipt signature verifies under the vessel's public key, and a go that was never warded answers `go-unsigned`.
* The residual: the receipt trust root names the daemon's vessel key.

!!! S6 ~ canon

This carrier gains its standing sections and rewrites the broker bullets and the trust-model line that name an in-memory key; the first owed row's in-memory-key residual retargets to the vessel-key residual; `kahua-memory-stage` closes the lift tension for the daemon and kahua wikis once the ward stands and keeps it open for user wikis; the road's `L22` row and the `F11` note follow.

<<~/ahu>>

